/E (     My interest in local finance emerged from a rural planning internship and was reinforced through coursework in poverty economics, particularly studies of microfinance in India as a response to usury under information asymmetry. In addition, recent issues surrounding the accumulation of local government financing vehicle \(LGFV\) debt have further motivated my interest in local finance and investment.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
545 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 112 0 R
/E (     Prior literature finds that lower returns in rural areas lead financial institutions to reallocate rural deposits to urban lending, resulting in negative investment that constrains rural income growth \(Wang & Li, 2013\). However, whether such capital outflows continue to restrict investment when rural households accumulate sufficient wealth remains unclear. Rising incomes may also intensify negative investment due to limited local investment opportunities, creating an interaction between income growth and capital outflows.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
546 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 112 0 R
/E (     Negative financial investment may also be reflected in LGFVs and local government bonds. While LGFV-funded infrastructure can generate positive spillovers for rural economies, it may also crowd out credit to households and local firms. This study therefore examines counties and rural areas, focusing on income heterogeneity and LGFV bond issuance to assess their effects on rural income growth)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
547 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 863 0 R
/E (Does the growth-inhibiting effect of negative investment vary across income thresholds and institutional conditions?)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
548 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 864 0 R
/E <486F7720646F20746865206475616C20726F6C6573206F66204C47465620626F6E647384696E6672617374727563747572652D64726976656E2067726F77746820616E64206372656469742063726F7764696E672D6F75748461666665637420727572616C20696E636F6D652067726F77746820696E2074686520726567696F6E3F>
